WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.680
Compare a sampled African genome and a sampled Eurasian genome with a Neanderthal

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.800
genome. The Eurasian genome shares more derived variants with the Neanderthal.

3
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.880
At each site, a chimpanzee shows which allele is ancestral, and a derived variant is

4
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.720
a newer one. Where does the surplus come from?

5
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.760
Two different histories could both explain it.

6
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:32.253
Count the sites. At an ABBA site, the Eurasian and the Neanderthal share the new

7
00:00:32.253 --> 00:00:36.813
variant. At a BABA site, the African and the Neanderthal share it.

8
00:00:37.593 --> 00:00:42.433
Suppose you count 600 ABBA sites and 400 BABA sites.

9
00:00:43.373 --> 00:00:47.173
D is the difference divided by the total, which comes to 0.20.

10
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:53.553
With no gene flow and one randomly mating ancestor, the model predicts equal counts,

11
00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:57.753
so D = 0. Here the Eurasian genome shares more.

12
00:00:58.573 --> 00:01:01.173
But do not read D as 20% % ancestry.

13
00:01:02.073 --> 00:01:06.553
Old structure in a subdivided African population can also make D positive.

14
00:01:07.513 --> 00:01:09.853
A positive D is consistent with gene flow.

15
00:01:10.713 --> 00:01:11.713
It is not proof.

16
00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:14.537
Time can separate the two stories.

17
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:20.737
Recombination breaks up DNA that entered from another population as it passes down

18
00:01:20.737 --> 00:01:24.597
the generations, so surviving pieces get shorter on average.

19
00:01:25.757 --> 00:01:30.637
Gene flow tens of thousands of years ago leaves long blocks, because recombination

20
00:01:30.637 --> 00:01:32.817
has had limited time to break them up.

21
00:01:33.577 --> 00:01:36.237
Old structure leaves much shorter pieces on average.

22
00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:39.777
So segment length carries information about timing.

23
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:47.070
Ust'-Ishim was a Homo sapiens man from western Siberia, who lived about 45,000 years

24
00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:52.390
ago. His genome carried Neanderthal segments longer than those in present-day

25
00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.890
people. Dated genomes should carry longer segments if gene flow happened shortly

26
00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:02.470
before they lived, and old structure predicts only a slight change with age.

27
00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:08.370
From the lengths, researchers inferred gene flow 7,000 to 13,000 years before he

28
00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:13.050
lived. That estimate carries the uncertainty of the recombination model.

29
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:18.490
Fit both histories as explicit models to the same data and compare them.

30
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:24.150
So the evidence supports Neanderthal gene flow into the ancestors of the sampled

31
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:29.110
non-Africans. Keep the conclusion to those samples, because it says nothing by

32
00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.570
itself about other contacts.

33
00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:32.790
A positive D is a clue.

34
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:34.810
Test it with segment length.
